1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,810
Meet Hiro.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:03,441
He makes small,

3
00:00:03,441 --> 00:00:06,075
precise metal parts in a workshop in

4
00:00:06,075 --> 00:00:08,156
a mountain town in Japan,

5
00:00:08,156 --> 00:00:10,263
parts that end up inside cars and

6
00:00:10,263 --> 00:00:12,970
machines all over the world.

7
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:14,551
For a long time now,

8
00:00:14,551 --> 00:00:16,478
the same paper sign has hung in

9
00:00:16,478 --> 00:00:17,800
his window.

10
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:19,290
Help wanted.

11
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:21,010
Nobody calls.

12
00:00:21,370 --> 00:00:23,100
Hiro isn't unlucky.

13
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:25,185
His whole country is running out of

14
00:00:25,185 --> 00:00:26,170
workers.

15
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:27,081
And yet,

16
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:29,128
a short drive from towns like his,

17
00:00:29,128 --> 00:00:31,978
Japan builds almost four in every ten

18
00:00:31,978 --> 00:00:34,650
industrial robots made on Earth.

19
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:37,044
So could the world's oldest big country

20
00:00:37,044 --> 00:00:39,775
turn its biggest problem into its biggest

21
00:00:39,775 --> 00:00:41,120
advantage?

22
00:00:51,983 --> 00:00:54,300
Hoshimachi isn't a real town,

23
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:56,382
but towns like it are all over

24
00:00:56,382 --> 00:00:57,503
Japan.

25
00:00:57,693 --> 00:00:59,693
The train comes less often.

26
00:00:59,693 --> 00:01:02,283
More shops keep their shutters down.

27
00:01:02,483 --> 00:01:04,974
Hiro's apprentice left for the city years

28
00:01:04,974 --> 00:01:05,631
ago,

29
00:01:05,631 --> 00:01:07,217
and his stool still sits by the

30
00:01:07,217 --> 00:01:08,043
lathe.

31
00:01:08,183 --> 00:01:09,202
The strange thing is,

32
00:01:09,202 --> 00:01:11,593
Hiro's customers aren't leaving.

33
00:01:11,593 --> 00:01:13,583
They want more parts than ever.

34
00:01:13,653 --> 00:01:16,133
What's missing is hands.

35
00:01:16,383 --> 00:01:18,643
Let me draw what happened.

36
00:01:18,643 --> 00:01:20,687
This bar is everyone of working age

37
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:21,485
in Japan,

38
00:01:21,485 --> 00:01:22,282
at the peak,

39
00:01:22,282 --> 00:01:24,283
a generation ago.

40
00:01:24,283 --> 00:01:26,183
This bar is today.

41
00:01:26,183 --> 00:01:27,129
Since that peak,

42
00:01:27,129 --> 00:01:29,553
Japan has lost about thirteen and a

43
00:01:29,553 --> 00:01:32,093
half million working-age people.

44
00:01:32,093 --> 00:01:34,240
That's almost as many people as live

45
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:35,913
in all of Tokyo.

46
00:01:35,993 --> 00:01:38,796
Picture every office and every train in

47
00:01:38,796 --> 00:01:39,422
Tokyo,

48
00:01:39,422 --> 00:01:40,423
empty.

49
00:01:40,423 --> 00:01:42,360
That's the size of the hole.

50
00:01:42,617 --> 00:01:44,917
And it isn't slowing down.

51
00:01:44,917 --> 00:01:46,903
The government's own population

52
00:01:46,903 --> 00:01:48,426
researchers project that,

53
00:01:48,426 --> 00:01:51,053
within the lifetime of today's children,

54
00:01:51,053 --> 00:01:53,260
Japan will have only about half the

55
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:56,067
working-age people it had at the peak.

56
00:01:56,327 --> 00:01:58,432
Think of a rush-hour train platform,

57
00:01:58,432 --> 00:02:00,687
where you can barely move.

58
00:02:00,757 --> 00:02:03,755
Now take away every other person.

59
00:02:04,017 --> 00:02:05,736
You can feel it in the job

60
00:02:05,736 --> 00:02:06,707
market.

61
00:02:06,707 --> 00:02:07,916
Month after month,

62
00:02:07,916 --> 00:02:10,137
Japan has had more open jobs than

63
00:02:10,137 --> 00:02:12,217
people looking for work.

64
00:02:12,307 --> 00:02:14,473
Hiro is competing for workers with the

65
00:02:14,473 --> 00:02:15,437
convenience store,

66
00:02:15,437 --> 00:02:18,577
the nursing home and the delivery company.

67
00:02:18,577 --> 00:02:20,759
And they're all losing too.

68
00:02:25,217 --> 00:02:28,227
Take the trucks that carry Hiro's parts.

69
00:02:28,227 --> 00:02:30,933
Japan capped truck drivers' overtime to

70
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:32,517
protect their health.

71
00:02:32,537 --> 00:02:34,237
Good for the drivers.

72
00:02:34,237 --> 00:02:35,701
But fewer drivers,

73
00:02:35,701 --> 00:02:37,166
working fewer hours,

74
00:02:37,166 --> 00:02:39,007
move fewer boxes.

75
00:02:39,137 --> 00:02:40,597
The government warns that,

76
00:02:40,597 --> 00:02:41,845
if nothing changes,

77
00:02:41,845 --> 00:02:43,749
by the end of the decade Japan

78
00:02:43,749 --> 00:02:45,441
could be short of trucks for about

79
00:02:45,441 --> 00:02:47,311
a third of its cargo.

80
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:50,010
Then there's Hiro's mother.

81
00:02:50,210 --> 00:02:51,570
She lives in a care home down

82
00:02:51,570 --> 00:02:52,510
the road.

83
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:55,350
It needs more caregivers every year,

84
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:57,644
because more people grow old and fewer

85
00:02:57,644 --> 00:02:59,850
young people arrive to help.

86
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,689
The health ministry's own estimate is that

87
00:03:02,689 --> 00:03:04,970
Japan will need hundreds of thousands more

88
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:07,623
caregivers in the years ahead.

89
00:03:07,883 --> 00:03:09,669
Even Hiro's granddaughter,

90
00:03:09,669 --> 00:03:10,099
Aoi,

91
00:03:10,099 --> 00:03:11,603
moved away.

92
00:03:11,643 --> 00:03:14,353
She writes software in Tokyo now.

93
00:03:14,413 --> 00:03:16,323
Hiro is proud of her.

94
00:03:16,353 --> 00:03:18,239
He still needs someone to run the

95
00:03:18,239 --> 00:03:19,074
lathe.

96
00:03:19,317 --> 00:03:20,120
So far,

97
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:22,889
this sounds like the story everyone tells

98
00:03:22,889 --> 00:03:24,597
about Japan.

99
00:03:24,667 --> 00:03:25,149
Old,

100
00:03:25,149 --> 00:03:25,884
shrinking,

101
00:03:25,884 --> 00:03:26,477
stuck.

102
00:03:26,477 --> 00:03:29,287
But here's the twist.

103
00:03:29,327 --> 00:03:31,353
The same kind of shortage that is

104
00:03:31,353 --> 00:03:34,139
emptying Hoshimachi helped create one of

105
00:03:34,139 --> 00:03:36,511
Japan's greatest industries.

106
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:43,840
Go back to nineteen sixty-nine.

107
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:46,953
Japan's economy was booming and factories

108
00:03:46,953 --> 00:03:48,750
couldn't hire fast enough.

109
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:49,838
Kawasaki,

110
00:03:49,838 --> 00:03:51,687
a heavy-industry company,

111
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:53,972
began building the first industrial robot

112
00:03:53,972 --> 00:03:54,990
made in Japan,

113
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:57,460
under an American license.

114
00:03:57,550 --> 00:04:00,324
Carmakers hungry for workers were among

115
00:04:00,324 --> 00:04:01,680
the first buyers.

116
00:04:01,917 --> 00:04:02,527
Today,

117
00:04:02,527 --> 00:04:05,471
the International Federation of Robotics

118
00:04:05,471 --> 00:04:07,785
calls Japan the world's predominant robot

119
00:04:07,785 --> 00:04:09,857
manufacturing country.

120
00:04:10,237 --> 00:04:10,972
FANUC,

121
00:04:10,972 --> 00:04:11,961
Yaskawa,

122
00:04:11,961 --> 00:04:14,749
Kawasaki and others build the arms that

123
00:04:14,749 --> 00:04:15,484
weld cars,

124
00:04:15,484 --> 00:04:18,526
assemble electronics and lift boxes on

125
00:04:18,526 --> 00:04:20,647
every continent.

126
00:04:20,787 --> 00:04:22,812
These are industrial robots,

127
00:04:22,812 --> 00:04:24,287
the workhorses.

128
00:04:24,287 --> 00:04:25,235
They don't walk,

129
00:04:25,235 --> 00:04:26,737
and they don't chat.

130
00:04:26,737 --> 00:04:28,306
They just work.

131
00:04:28,550 --> 00:04:31,010
But here's the strange part.

132
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:33,570
Look at where the robots go.

133
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:35,831
In its latest fiscal year,

134
00:04:35,831 --> 00:04:38,645
nearly nine of every ten dollars of

135
00:04:38,645 --> 00:04:41,458
FANUC's sales came from customers outside

136
00:04:41,458 --> 00:04:42,570
Japan.

137
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:45,590
Its robot sales in China jumped.

138
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:48,320
Its robot sales at home fell.

139
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:50,530
Japan builds the robots.

140
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:52,346
The world buys them.

141
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:55,663
So who actually uses the most robots?

142
00:04:55,663 --> 00:04:57,896
Count robots per factory worker,

143
00:04:57,896 --> 00:04:59,700
and South Korea has more than twice

144
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,653
as many as Japan.

145
00:05:01,673 --> 00:05:04,097
Singapore and Germany are ahead of Japan

146
00:05:04,097 --> 00:05:04,883
too.

147
00:05:04,903 --> 00:05:07,457
The robot superpower is not the most

148
00:05:07,457 --> 00:05:09,218
robotic country.

149
00:05:09,483 --> 00:05:11,730
Why would a shortage be good news

150
00:05:11,730 --> 00:05:13,173
for robots?

151
00:05:13,173 --> 00:05:14,593
Let me draw it.

152
00:05:14,773 --> 00:05:17,521
Fewer workers means employers compete for

153
00:05:17,521 --> 00:05:18,044
them,

154
00:05:18,044 --> 00:05:19,743
so wages rise.

155
00:05:19,813 --> 00:05:21,940
Higher wages make a robot look cheap

156
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:23,603
by comparison.

157
00:05:23,733 --> 00:05:25,562
So companies buy robots,

158
00:05:25,562 --> 00:05:29,173
and every remaining worker produces more.

159
00:05:29,423 --> 00:05:32,353
That last box is the whole game.

160
00:05:32,453 --> 00:05:34,133
Output per worker.

161
00:05:34,243 --> 00:05:36,574
A country can shrink and still get

162
00:05:36,574 --> 00:05:38,293
richer per person,

163
00:05:38,293 --> 00:05:40,932
if each pair of hands does more.

164
00:05:45,383 --> 00:05:47,392
Step behind the drink fridge in a

165
00:05:47,392 --> 00:05:49,124
Tokyo convenience store,

166
00:05:49,124 --> 00:05:51,132
and you may find a robot arm

167
00:05:51,132 --> 00:05:54,243
sliding cold bottles onto the shelves.

168
00:05:54,503 --> 00:05:55,446
Its maker,

169
00:05:55,446 --> 00:05:57,839
a startup called Telexistence,

170
00:05:57,839 --> 00:06:00,014
says one arm can restock about a

171
00:06:00,014 --> 00:06:01,823
thousand drinks a day.

172
00:06:02,153 --> 00:06:03,722
When it fumbles a bottle,

173
00:06:03,722 --> 00:06:05,709
a person somewhere else puts on a

174
00:06:05,709 --> 00:06:08,693
headset and finishes the job remotely.

175
00:06:08,913 --> 00:06:10,443
That's not a demo.

176
00:06:10,503 --> 00:06:12,627
That's a deployment.

177
00:06:12,883 --> 00:06:15,188
Now compare that with the robots in

178
00:06:15,188 --> 00:06:16,813
the headlines.

179
00:06:16,843 --> 00:06:19,534
Japan's most famous walking humanoid,

180
00:06:19,534 --> 00:06:20,990
Honda's ASIMO,

181
00:06:20,990 --> 00:06:23,211
charmed crowds for years and retired

182
00:06:23,211 --> 00:06:26,033
without ever getting a real job.

183
00:06:26,153 --> 00:06:26,839
Today,

184
00:06:26,839 --> 00:06:29,914
Seven-Eleven Japan and Telexistence are

185
00:06:29,914 --> 00:06:32,492
developing a humanoid helper,

186
00:06:32,492 --> 00:06:34,621
with a target of putting it in

187
00:06:34,621 --> 00:06:36,843
stores by twenty twenty-nine.

188
00:06:36,843 --> 00:06:37,253
That's a plan,

189
00:06:37,253 --> 00:06:38,273
not a product.

190
00:06:38,383 --> 00:06:39,463
In Japan,

191
00:06:39,463 --> 00:06:41,678
the robots doing the work right now

192
00:06:41,678 --> 00:06:42,481
are arms,

193
00:06:42,481 --> 00:06:43,911
not androids.

194
00:06:44,150 --> 00:06:45,890
Back at the care home,

195
00:06:45,890 --> 00:06:48,260
the helpers are quieter.

196
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:50,513
Sensors under the mattress that tell night

197
00:06:50,513 --> 00:06:52,220
staff who is awake.

198
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:54,642
Machines that help lift residents out of

199
00:06:54,642 --> 00:06:55,450
bed.

200
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:58,171
Economists who studied Japanese nursing

201
00:06:58,171 --> 00:07:00,640
homes found something surprising.

202
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:03,755
Homes that adopted robots ended up with

203
00:07:03,755 --> 00:07:04,457
more staff,

204
00:07:04,457 --> 00:07:05,401
not fewer,

205
00:07:05,401 --> 00:07:07,822
and caregivers spent more of their time

206
00:07:07,822 --> 00:07:10,843
on what the researchers call human touch.

207
00:07:11,083 --> 00:07:13,783
Which brings us back to Hiro.

208
00:07:13,783 --> 00:07:14,677
One weekend,

209
00:07:14,677 --> 00:07:16,803
Aoi comes home with a plan.

210
00:07:16,803 --> 00:07:18,173
Not a humanoid.

211
00:07:18,173 --> 00:07:20,700
A small collaborative robot arm,

212
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:23,019
the kind built to work safely beside

213
00:07:23,019 --> 00:07:24,083
people.

214
00:07:24,183 --> 00:07:25,553
They name it Kobo,

215
00:07:25,553 --> 00:07:28,243
from the Japanese word for workshop.

216
00:07:28,313 --> 00:07:30,383
Aoi writes the program.

217
00:07:30,383 --> 00:07:31,744
Kobo loads the lathe,

218
00:07:31,744 --> 00:07:33,104
again and again,

219
00:07:33,104 --> 00:07:35,411
while Hiro does the part only he

220
00:07:35,411 --> 00:07:36,079
can do,

221
00:07:36,079 --> 00:07:38,847
checking every piece with a lifetime of

222
00:07:38,847 --> 00:07:40,139
experience.

223
00:07:40,383 --> 00:07:42,993
But who gets paid for Kobo?

224
00:07:42,993 --> 00:07:44,453
Let me draw it.

225
00:07:44,483 --> 00:07:44,973
First,

226
00:07:44,973 --> 00:07:45,720
the arm,

227
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:47,093
the hardware.

228
00:07:47,103 --> 00:07:49,203
Japan is superb at this.

229
00:07:49,203 --> 00:07:50,005
Second,

230
00:07:50,005 --> 00:07:50,806
the brain,

231
00:07:50,806 --> 00:07:53,294
the AI software that lets a robot

232
00:07:53,294 --> 00:07:54,723
see and learn.

233
00:07:54,833 --> 00:07:56,837
Much of that is written in America

234
00:07:56,837 --> 00:07:57,983
and China.

235
00:07:58,023 --> 00:07:59,060
And finally,

236
00:07:59,060 --> 00:07:59,885
the user,

237
00:07:59,885 --> 00:08:02,000
the small business that puts the robot

238
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,023
to work.

239
00:08:03,173 --> 00:08:04,733
In an AI economy,

240
00:08:04,733 --> 00:08:06,313
more and more of the money flows

241
00:08:06,313 --> 00:08:08,493
to the brain and to the user.

242
00:08:08,493 --> 00:08:10,922
Japan owns the arm.

243
00:08:15,383 --> 00:08:17,303
Now the catches.

244
00:08:17,303 --> 00:08:17,445
First,

245
00:08:17,445 --> 00:08:18,023
the currency.

246
00:08:18,023 --> 00:08:19,532
Since the pandemic,

247
00:08:19,532 --> 00:08:21,835
Japan's money has lost more than a

248
00:08:21,835 --> 00:08:24,943
quarter of its value against the dollar.

249
00:08:24,943 --> 00:08:27,396
That makes Japanese robots cheap for

250
00:08:27,396 --> 00:08:28,352
foreign buyers,

251
00:08:28,352 --> 00:08:29,933
which helps FANUC.

252
00:08:30,053 --> 00:08:32,540
But it makes every imported chip,

253
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:35,256
every barrel of fuel and every software

254
00:08:35,256 --> 00:08:37,946
subscription more expensive for Hiro.

255
00:08:38,183 --> 00:08:39,142
Second,

256
00:08:39,142 --> 00:08:40,303
software.

257
00:08:40,303 --> 00:08:42,436
Japan builds superb machines,

258
00:08:42,436 --> 00:08:45,073
but the economy around them hasn't kept

259
00:08:45,073 --> 00:08:45,913
up.

260
00:08:46,183 --> 00:08:48,608
Measured by output per hour worked,

261
00:08:48,608 --> 00:08:50,611
Japan sits in the bottom third of

262
00:08:50,611 --> 00:08:52,323
the rich-country club.

263
00:08:52,483 --> 00:08:55,183
And remember FANUC's robot sales at home?

264
00:08:55,183 --> 00:08:56,413
They fell.

265
00:08:56,443 --> 00:08:58,633
The robots are being made here.

266
00:08:58,643 --> 00:09:00,539
They just aren't spreading fast enough

267
00:09:00,539 --> 00:09:01,349
here.

268
00:09:01,583 --> 00:09:03,763
And then there is debt.

269
00:09:03,773 --> 00:09:06,465
The IMF says Japan's government owes about

270
00:09:06,465 --> 00:09:09,135
twice the size of its entire economy,

271
00:09:09,135 --> 00:09:12,203
the highest of any advanced country.

272
00:09:12,283 --> 00:09:14,925
The government also owns large financial

273
00:09:14,925 --> 00:09:15,621
assets,

274
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:18,183
so the net picture is less extreme.

275
00:09:18,183 --> 00:09:20,111
But as more people retire,

276
00:09:20,111 --> 00:09:22,307
more money goes to pensions and health

277
00:09:22,307 --> 00:09:22,771
care,

278
00:09:22,771 --> 00:09:26,055
and there's less room for everything else.

279
00:09:26,317 --> 00:09:27,691
Add it all up,

280
00:09:27,691 --> 00:09:29,933
and you can see why so many

281
00:09:29,933 --> 00:09:33,507
videos call Japan a slow-motion collapse.

282
00:09:33,507 --> 00:09:34,201
Last year,

283
00:09:34,201 --> 00:09:37,310
its whole economy grew barely more than

284
00:09:37,310 --> 00:09:38,507
one percent.

285
00:09:38,507 --> 00:09:39,282
And yet,

286
00:09:39,282 --> 00:09:41,956
that headline may be measuring the wrong

287
00:09:41,956 --> 00:09:42,823
thing.

288
00:09:47,283 --> 00:09:48,786
Economists led by Jesús

289
00:09:48,786 --> 00:09:51,577
Fernández-Villaverde of the University of

290
00:09:51,577 --> 00:09:54,753
Pennsylvania asked a different question.

291
00:09:55,093 --> 00:09:57,718
Not how fast Japan grew per citizen,

292
00:09:57,718 --> 00:10:00,003
but per working-age adult.

293
00:10:00,143 --> 00:10:01,313
Their answer?

294
00:10:01,453 --> 00:10:04,137
From the late nineties until the pandemic,

295
00:10:04,137 --> 00:10:07,233
Japan grew slightly faster than America.

296
00:10:07,483 --> 00:10:09,353
Let me draw it.

297
00:10:09,353 --> 00:10:11,722
Japan's total economy looks flat,

298
00:10:11,722 --> 00:10:13,678
because the number of workers keeps

299
00:10:13,678 --> 00:10:14,713
falling.

300
00:10:14,843 --> 00:10:17,265
But divide by the people who actually

301
00:10:17,265 --> 00:10:17,683
work,

302
00:10:17,683 --> 00:10:18,762
and the line climbs,

303
00:10:18,762 --> 00:10:21,123
right alongside America's.

304
00:10:21,333 --> 00:10:24,353
Japan's problem was never lazy workers.

305
00:10:24,353 --> 00:10:26,093
It was fewer of them.

306
00:10:26,213 --> 00:10:28,842
And fewer hands is exactly the problem

307
00:10:28,842 --> 00:10:30,875
robots are built to solve.

308
00:10:31,117 --> 00:10:31,693
So,

309
00:10:31,693 --> 00:10:34,717
will AI make Japan rich again?

310
00:10:34,767 --> 00:10:36,299
Not the way it was in the

311
00:10:36,299 --> 00:10:36,934
bubble,

312
00:10:36,934 --> 00:10:38,685
when Tokyo land was said to be

313
00:10:38,685 --> 00:10:40,817
worth more than entire countries.

314
00:10:40,927 --> 00:10:43,013
That boom was built on debt and

315
00:10:43,013 --> 00:10:44,327
land prices.

316
00:10:44,327 --> 00:10:44,979
This one,

317
00:10:44,979 --> 00:10:46,080
if it comes,

318
00:10:46,080 --> 00:10:47,878
would be built on output per pair

319
00:10:47,878 --> 00:10:48,747
of hands.

320
00:10:48,747 --> 00:10:50,959
Robots and AI can let a shrinking

321
00:10:50,959 --> 00:10:54,017
Japan keep getting richer per person.

322
00:10:54,027 --> 00:10:56,233
But only if small firms like Hiro's

323
00:10:56,233 --> 00:10:57,680
actually use them,

324
00:10:57,680 --> 00:10:59,886
Japan writes more of the brains and

325
00:10:59,886 --> 00:11:01,087
not just the arms,

326
00:11:01,087 --> 00:11:03,539
and the debt doesn't crowd everything else

327
00:11:03,539 --> 00:11:04,384
out.

328
00:11:04,650 --> 00:11:06,078
In Hoshimachi,

329
00:11:06,078 --> 00:11:08,256
the help-wanted sign is still in the

330
00:11:08,256 --> 00:11:09,340
window.

331
00:11:09,510 --> 00:11:11,950
Hiro still wants an apprentice.

332
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:14,265
But now the parts ship on time,

333
00:11:14,265 --> 00:11:16,754
and Aoi comes home to teach Kobo

334
00:11:16,754 --> 00:11:18,460
something new.

335
00:11:18,830 --> 00:11:22,170
Japan's biggest problem isn't going away.

336
00:11:22,350 --> 00:11:25,102
What's changing is what a workshop with

337
00:11:25,102 --> 00:11:27,231
too few hands can do.

338
00:11:27,483 --> 00:11:28,188
Next time,

339
00:11:28,188 --> 00:11:31,348
we follow the money behind another hidden

340
00:11:31,348 --> 00:11:33,893
link of the world economy.

341
00:11:34,013 --> 00:11:36,625
This is The Hidden Economics.
